## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical gating of Terraform plans using a dedicated QA backend, plan export, and automated validation to prevent prod touches.

## Key Concepts
- Backend aliasing and per-env state isolation
- terraform plan output in JSON
- Scripting plan validation with jq/Node.js
- CI gating and manual approvals for apply

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js snippet to validate plan.json plan boundaries
const fs=require('fs');
const plan=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('plan.json','utf8'));
const resources=plan?.planned_values?.root_module?.resources||[];
const ok=resources.every(r=>r.address.startsWith('modules/qa/')||r.address.includes('environment=qa'));
process.exit(ok?0:1);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to multi-module repos?
- How do you handle drift checks beyond plan validation?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world Terraform governance requires strict isolation and policy enforcement when multiple teams/environments share global resources. This question probes your ability to combine per-environment workspaces with policy guardrails to prevent cross-environment impact.

## Key Concepts
- Per-environment Terraform Cloud workspaces for isolation
- Provider aliases to target the correct AWS account
- Sentinel policy to guard changes to shared VPC components
- CI validation: parse plan JSON and enforce env-tag constraints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sentinel-like illustrative policy (Terraform Cloud Sentinel syntax would differ)
policy "env_guard" {
  rule deny_shared_vpc_changes {
    // deny any change to shared VPC unless resource.tags.env == workspace.name
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle tag drift across environments?
- How would you test policy changes locally before pushing to CI?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests multi-account Terraform patterns: provider aliases, cross-account data sources, and deterministic apply order.

## Key Concepts

- Aliased providers
- Cross-account data sources
- Data referencing across providers
- depends_on for ordering
- Credentials management via profiles and assume_role

## Code Example

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias   = "prod"
  region  = "us-east-1"
  profile = "prod"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias   = "audit"
  region  = "us-east-1"
  profile = "audit"
}

data "aws_caller_identity" "prod" {
  provider = aws.prod
}

data "aws_s3_bucket" "prod_bucket" {
  provider = aws.prod
  bucket   = "my-prod-bucket"
}

data "aws_iam_policy_document" "audit" {
  provider = aws.audit
  statement {
    actions   = ["s3:GetObject"]
    resources = ["${data.aws_s3_bucket.prod_bucket.arn}/*"]
    principals {
      type        = "AWS"
      identifiers = ["arn:aws:iam::${data.aws_caller_identity.prod.account_id}:root"]
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "audit_bucket" {
  provider = aws.audit
  bucket   = "audit-bucket"
}

resource "aws_iam_policy" "audit_policy" {
  provider = aws.audit
  name     = "CrossAccountGetObject"
  policy   = data.aws_iam_policy_document.audit.json
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate credentials for the aliased providers safely?
- How would you test this cross-account permission in a CI workflow?
